---
title: Email Templates
description: Create beautiful, branded emails with custom templates.
---

FOSSBilling uses [Twig](https://twig.symfony.com/) for email templates, so you can control both the wording and the presentation of system messages.

## Managing Templates

### Access Email Settings

In the admin sidebar, go to **System → Settings**, select **Email**, and open the **Email Templates** tab.

You'll see every installed email template, along with its subject, owning module, and enabled status.

### Reset Built-In Templates

Use **Bulk Actions → Regenerate Built-in Templates** to restore the default subject and body of existing built-in templates. This overwrites any edits to those fields. Save a copy of your edits before running the reset.

## Editing Templates

1. Open the template with **Edit**, then change the subject and content.
2. Select **Preview** to render the current draft with example values.
3. Select **Update** to save your changes.

![Editing an email template](../../../assets/guides/emails/template-edit.png)

### Available Variables

Templates can use any variables passed by the module. Common ones include:

- `staff_member_name` — Staff recipient's name in the built-in test email
- `c.first_name` — Recipient's first name in client emails
- `guest.system_company.name` — Your company name
- `guest.system_email.signature` — The [configured email signature](/admin-guide/company/#signatures)

The **Variables** tab shows the example values used by **Preview**. If the tab lists no variables, trigger the action that sends the template in a test installation.

To check email delivery, use **Send a Test Email to Yourself** in Email settings. It sends the built-in test template to your staff address.

Email templates run in a Twig sandbox. Use [supported tags, filters, and functions](https://github.com/FOSSBilling/FOSSBilling/blob/a480f5fc28991f3d27e5641b5e7b011e269504a4/src/library/FOSSBilling/Twig/EmailPolicy.php#L25-L54) such as `if`, `format_date`, and `markdown_to_html`.

## Template Examples

These examples show two ways to format the built-in test email.

### Markdown Format

Use `apply markdown_to_html` to render Markdown. Place the formatted signature after that block.

```twig {% process=false %}
{% apply markdown_to_html %}
Hi {{ staff_member_name|default('') }},

If you are reading this email, FOSSBilling is **configured properly** and is **able to send emails**.
{% endapply %}

{{ guest.system_email.signature }}
```

### HTML Format

```twig {% process=false %}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <style>
    body {
      font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
      font-size: 14px;
      color: #333333;
    }
    h1 {
      font-size: 24px;
      margin: 0 0 20px;
    }
    .signature {
      font-style: italic;
      color: #999999;
      margin-top: 20px;
      border-top: 1px solid #cccccc;
      padding-top: 10px;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <h1>Test email from {{ guest.system_company.name }}</h1>
  <p>Hi {{ staff_member_name|default('') }},</p>
  <p>If you are reading this email, FOSSBilling is <strong>configured properly</strong>.</p>
  <div class="signature">{{ guest.system_email.signature }}</div>
</body>
</html>
```

